(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608132600")
  (:adr/title "Eight of the ten standing detector findings were stale keys, not defects")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-13")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/body "ADR-2608131800 registered four more detectors into scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs and
ended by recommending the launchd plist NOT be installed, for one reason: ten findings were standing
red, so the first scheduled report would have been entirely STANDING, which ADR-2608124800 and
ADR-2608125000 treat as indistinguishable from silence. This is that triage.

## Result in one line

Eight of the ten were the DETECTOR OR ITS DATA being stale, not the code being wrong. Seven of those
eight were one mechanism repeated seven times: an exemption list keyed on west path, and the paths
moved. Two findings are real, understood, and are now recorded as ACCEPTED rather than left red.

## Verdicts

### 1. verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover — jvm-dep-not-frozen:orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/deps.edn

VERDICT: stale contract data. The frozen inventory in 90-docs/migration/kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover.edn
names orgs/kotoba-lang/compiler/deps.edn for the entry :compiler-clojure-bootstrap. That repository was
RENAMED to kotoba-lang/amu. Evidence: gh api repos/kotoba-lang/compiler and gh api repos/kotoba-lang/amu
both return id 1297097065 — one upstream, two names. amu did not acquire a JVM dependency; the same
bootstrap debt (org.clojure/clojure 1.12.0) is under the repository's new name.

Independent corroboration, from a third file written for another purpose:
orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang/src/kotoba/lang/grammar_authority.clj says, of the same repository, that
it was renamed and a checkout may still be under either name, and lists both ../amu and ../compiler
as mirror paths.

FIX: retargeted the ONE existing entry to the repository's current path. The inventory still freezes
exactly four entries — this is not a widening, and widening was explicitly not the remedy. The scan
list in the verifier already said amu; only the contract was behind.

Why nothing caught it: the verifier's legacy-path-missing check tests existsSync on the inventory
path, and a stale checkout of the OLD name is still on disk, so the path existed and the check passed.

CHECK STRENGTHENED (not weakened): added inventory-path-not-scanned, which fails when the frozen
inventory names a dependency file the verifier never opens. That is the defect itself — a freeze with
no subject — stated directly instead of surfacing as a confusing accusation against amu.

EVIDENCE, red before:
  problems=1 / exit 1 / FINDING jvm-dep-not-frozen:orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/deps.edn
EVIDENCE, green after:
  problems=0 / exit 0 / SCANNED 9 contract/dependency path(s)
  (SCANNED fell 10 to 9 because the inventory and the scan list now name the same paths.)
EVIDENCE, the new check fires on the real defect — reverted the inventory to the old repo name:
  problems=2 / exit 1, BOTH
    FINDING inventory-path-not-scanned:orgs/kotoba-lang/compiler/deps.edn
    FINDING jvm-dep-not-frozen:orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/deps.edn
  restored: problems=0 / exit 0.
EVIDENCE, the ORIGINAL check still catches what it was for — removed the :kototama-chicory-tender entry
so a scanned repo carries a JVM/Chicory dependency the inventory does not cover:
  FINDING jvm-dep-not-frozen:orgs/kotoba-lang/kototama/deps.edn — fires. Restored: green.

### 2 and 3. verify-kotoba-host-parity — import-sets-drifted, matrix-vs-implementation

VERDICT: both real. ACCEPTED, not fixed. One of the two was also 65 percent detector staleness, which
is fixed.

AUTHORITY, established before touching anything, and not from the file's own claim to be an SSoT:
orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang/src/kotoba/lang/grammar_authority.clj declares lang/host-parity.edn the
authority and enumerates its copies (resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn and
../kotoba/resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn) as vendored mirrors. That is an independent mechanism,
in a third file, written for a different reason (drift detection over vendored authorities), so it is
evidence rather than self-assertion. The reading that follows from it: the catalogue is the authority
for what is ALLOWED; kototama's contract.cljc and wasm-webcomponent's actor-host.js are ground truth
for what EXISTS; where they disagree, the catalogue is what moves.

Measured while establishing that: there is a FOURTH copy, and it has already drifted. sha256 of
lang/host-parity.edn and resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn agree (69d66b3b...); the copy in
kotoba-lang/kotoba is 0cfe8556... . grammar_authority's own docstring says host-parity in kotoba was
surveyed on 2026-08-10 and NOT checked.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG, enumerated:
  - 5 imports exist in BOTH implementations that the catalogue does not declare at all:
    :http-fetch :cbor-encode :json-encode :json-extract-field :http-post-headers. They reached the
    browser on 2026-07-25 (wasm-webcomponent 498e149). The catalogue's :unlisted-import-default is
    {:browser :no}, so a guest author reading the authority believes cbor-encode is browser-absent
    while the browser host implements it unconditionally. This is disclosure owed, not a capability
    to widen.
  - 4 more exist in contract.cljc only, JVM-side: :http-get-stream :object-get-stream :object-put-block
    :object-compare-and-set-ref.
  - 10 are declared with :component-link status and are not actor:host :import/id entries at all:
    :http-open :http-write :http-read :http-close :http-get :db-open :db-write :db-read :db-close
    :db-exchange.
  - 1 overclaim, the sharpest item: the catalogue gives :http-get {:browser :inject} and carries a
    conformance case :http-get-browser-inject-available expecting :available — and actor-host.js does
    not implement http_get at all. It is not even in IMPORT_SURFACE. That is exactly the lie the
    catalogue's own note forbids: guests must not assume an import is ambient just because it compiles.

DETECTOR STALENESS FIXED: matrix-vs-implementation reported 17 ids. Eleven of them were the verifier's
own hand-written not-browser-callable set being out of date — the pg wire and pool functions added to
actor-host.js on 2026-07-31 (Node pg-pool/wire/scram inject fail-closed) were never added to it.
actor-host.js is ONE file serving two hosts, so fns.<x> = does not mean callable in a browser, and
nothing in the file's syntax separates them; a hand-maintained discriminator is the only thing
available, so it is now CHECKED rather than trusted. Two new assertions:
  exclusion-list-names-unimplemented   — an excluded id actor-host.js no longer implements
  exclusion-list-contradicts-matrix    — an excluded id the catalogue claims is browser-available
Both directions matter because a stale exclusion SUBTRACTS an id from the comparison, which is the
direction that makes a check pass rather than fail.

EVIDENCE, before: 17 ids
  matrix-only #{:http-get}
  implementation-only 16 ids including pg-open, pg-query, pg-simple-query and all 8 pg-pool-*
EVIDENCE, after: 6 ids, all real
  matrix-only #{:http-get}
  implementation-only #{:http-post-headers :json-extract-field :json-encode :http-fetch :cbor-encode}
EVIDENCE, both new guards fire, tested against a COPY of the three sources in a scratch fixture so no
shared checkout was written:
  removed fns.pg_pool_close from the copied actor-host.js
    -> FINDING exclusion-list-names-unimplemented {:absent #{:pg-pool-close}}
  flipped :pg-pool-close :browser :no to :inject in the copied catalogue
    -> FINDING exclusion-list-contradicts-matrix {:ids {:pg-pool-close :inject}}
  unmodified control before and after both breaks: 2 findings, the 6-id result.

WHY ACCEPTED RATHER THAN FIXED. Correcting the catalogue is a three-repository edit to a capability
surface. It re-baselines counts the file itself guards (browser-linkable-count-changed asserts exactly
10; :honest-gaps carries :global-browser-parity-10-of-59), it retires or inverts a conformance case, it
changes :required-imports from 59 to 68, and it has to carry a fourth copy that has ALREADY drifted for
reasons nobody has looked at. Landing that blind is how a half-fix gets recorded as a fix. The residual
is small and exactly named, which is the deliverable here.

### 4 through 10. verify-layer-deps — the seven internal-numbered-dir findings

VERDICT: all seven are a gap in how the checker models the workspace, and the registry's own :why for
this detector was WRONG about them. It said the seven were numbered directories re-created inside three
app repos after layers.edn froze the list. Not one of them was.

manifest/layers.edn keys its exemption lists (:grandfathered-internal-dirs, :frozen-legacy-roots) on
WEST PATH. Three GitHub org-transfers and one rename moved the paths; the exemptions did not follow.
The exemption did not lapse — its address changed — but the output is indistinguishable from a repo
that added a numbered directory yesterday.

  gftdcojp/app-aozora            -> network-awai/app-aozora     gh id 1275051908
  gftdcojp/club-shinshi          -> network-awai/club-shinshi   gh id 1281275923
  gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp -> gftdcojp/apps-gftdcojp      gh id 1136866292
  etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-kenchi -> cloud-itonami/kenchi        gh id 1282142295

Identity was confirmed by GitHub repo id, not by name similarity. Age was confirmed independently:
git log --diff-filter=A dates every one of the seven directories to 2026-04-08 through 2026-06-26 —
all BEFORE layers.edn froze the list on 2026-07-17. So they are the same directories that were
grandfathered, under paths that moved.

FIX: layers.edn now names the current west paths. The set of exempt UPSTREAM repositories is unchanged,
which is the test that this is a repair and not a widening; a comment in the file now states that rule
(the gh id must match, or it is a new repo being exempted, which the list must never do).

One extra entry: orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp is still on disk as an orphan checkout of the same
upstream (tip of 2026-07-20, not registered in west.yml, which registers only apps-gftdcojp). The
checker walks the disk, not west, so listing only one path guarantees the other fires. Both are listed,
with a comment saying the orphan's removal belongs to ADR-2608122128's orphan repair and that the line
goes when the checkout does.

EVIDENCE, red before: 7 violations, exit 1 without --report
  internal-numbered-dir at orgs/gftdcojp/apps-gftdcojp {00-contracts, 50-infra, 70-tools}
  internal-numbered-dir at orgs/network-awai/app-aozora {00-contracts, 70-tools}
  internal-numbered-dir at orgs/network-awai/club-shinshi {00-contracts, 70-tools}
EVIDENCE, green after: OK: no layer violations / SCANNED 4415 / exit 0, run WITHOUT --report so a
violation would have exited 1.
EVIDENCE, the check still catches a genuinely new numbered directory: built a scratch tree with
orgs/testorg/app-brandnew/{00-contracts,90-docs}, on no exemption list, and pointed the checker at it
via LAYERS_EDN:
  1 violation, FINDING internal-numbered-dir:orgs/testorg/app-brandnew:00-contracts, exit 1
  — and 90-docs correctly ignored, since it is in :repo-internal-allowed.

The registry's :why for this detector has been corrected in place, with the correction marked, because
a wrong :why is worse than a missing one: it is a plausible story written from the finding text rather
than from the repositories, and it is what a later reader would trust instead of measuring.

## The mechanism, stated once

Six of the ten (one cutover plus five of the seven layer-deps) and arguably all eight have ONE shape:
a workspace fact keyed by PATH, and GitHub moved the path. A rename or an org transfer leaves every
by-path list — frozen inventories, exemption lists, scan lists, mirror lists — pointing at an address
that no longer holds the thing. The finding that results is real (that path is not exempt / not frozen)
and its cause is invisible (the exemption never lapsed). Two of the by-path lists in this repository
had ALREADY solved it and one had not: grammar_authority.clj lists both ../amu and ../compiler and says
why; layers.edn and the cutover contract did not. The general remedy is to record repo identity
(GitHub id) next to any by-path key that grants an exemption, so a later reader can tell a moved
exemption from a new violation without running gh api.

## The registry had no way to say ACCEPTED, so one was added

An accepted finding had nowhere to go. With only NEW / RESOLVED / STANDING it decays into STANDING —
the class this whole design calls indistinguishable from silence — so a decision to accept would have
been recorded nowhere and would read, a month later, exactly like a defect nobody opened.

Added, as the smallest thing that works:
  - manifest/orgs-detectors.edn: an OPTIONAL :accepted map on an entry, finding-key ->
    {:since :why :clears-when}, all three non-empty strings.
  - scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs: admission REJECTS a malformed :accepted (fail-closed, exit 2, the
    whole tick, consistent with the other admission rules). merge-findings takes the accepted set;
    an accepted key is never reported NEW and never counted in STANDING. An acceptance whose finding
    is ABSENT is reported loudly as a STALE ACCEPTANCE.
  - .claude/hooks/session-start-orgs-detectors.cljs: accepted N on its own line, never enumerated;
    stale acceptance is loud and marks the whole report loud.

Two things stop this becoming a way to make reds disappear. Accepting costs as much writing as fixing
a small thing, because admission demands a date, a reason and an exit condition. And an exemption that
outlives its subject is announced, because otherwise it silently exempts whatever lands on that key
next.

EVIDENCE:
  --self-test: 12 assertions -> 19, adding accepted-is-not-new, accepted-is-tracked-not-deleted,
  present-acceptance, and stale-acceptance-when-the-finding-goes-away. Breaking the implementation
  (stop excluding accepted keys from new-keys) produced SELF-TEST FAIL: an accepted finding is not
  announced as new, exit 1 — an assertion firing, not a reader throwing.
  Through the real tick, twice, against a scratch state file: ACCEPTED 2 on both runs, zero NEW.
  Break: an :accepted key with no live finding produced STALE ACCEPTANCE with its :since date.
  Break: an :accepted entry missing :clears-when produced ADMISSION FAILED and ran no detector.
  Hook render: accepted 2 on its own line, and the detector NOT counted in standing.

## Installation recommendation

INSTALL IT. The condition ADR-2608131800 set — do not install while the first report would be entirely
STANDING — no longer holds for the three detectors this triage covers.

What a tick costs, from the recorded state of the 2026-08-12 baseline run on this machine, at the load
that machine actually carries (60 to 150; the same detector was measured at 26.5s at load 1 and 2m06s
at load 133, so treat every number here as a loaded number):

  every 6h  verify-vendored-copies 101s + verify-layer-deps 37s + verify-kotoba-host-parity 3s
            + verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover 3s  =  about 2.4 minutes
  every 24h the above plus verify-require-graph 480s + verify-tsukuru-factory-plane 822s
            =  about 24 minutes, once a day
  Detectors are read-only, run with GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=none, and the tick writes only under
  ~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/.

What the first report after landing would say, for the three detectors triaged:
  verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover  clean
  verify-layer-deps                   clean
  verify-kotoba-host-parity           accepted 2, standing 0, new 0

What remains standing, named, and NOT triaged here because it belongs to other work:
  verify-vendored-copies   13 findings — ADR-2608130701 triaged these the same day
  verify-require-graph     99 findings — its own :measured records 2 orphan namespaces that gate and
                           97 report-only sibling gaps that do not; the split is not visible in the
                           finding stream and that is the next thing worth fixing in this home
  verify-tsukuru-factory-plane  0 findings

So the first report is not entirely STANDING, and it is not entirely clean either — it says two things
are known and accepted with a written exit, and 112 findings sit in two detectors whose own triage is
recorded elsewhere. That is a report that means something, which was the condition.

NOT DONE, deliberately: the launchd plist was not installed or loaded. That remains the owner's
decision, and scripts/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick.plist states in its header what runs, when, and what
it touches.

## Follow-ups, in priority order

  1. kotoba-lang PR correcting lang/host-parity.edn: declare the 5 browser-implemented codec/http
     imports and the 4 JVM-only ones, reclassify the 10 :component-link entries, and set :http-get
     :browser to :no or implement http_get in actor-host.js. Re-sync BOTH mirrors, including the
     already-drifted copy in kotoba-lang/kotoba. Advance the west pin. Update this repository's
     (= 10 (count browser-linkable)) qualification in the same change. That clears both accepted
     findings, and the acceptances must then be DELETED from the registry — the tick will say so.
  2. verify-require-graph: separate the 2 gating findings from the 97 report-only ones in the FINDING
     severity field, so 99 standing does not hide 2 that matter.
  3. Orphan checkout orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp — remove under ADR-2608122128, then drop its
     line from layers.edn :frozen-legacy-roots.
  4. Record GitHub repo id alongside by-path exemption keys, so the next rename is legible without
     gh api.")))
